  "query_preview": "..\n(Thursday through next Monday)\nIssued at 102 PM CST Tue Nov 17 2026\n\nSouthwesterly flow continues aloft at the forefront of the\nextended. This regime will be interrupted by another upper trough\nthat moves across the central CONUS Friday and Saturday. This\ntrough is forecast to be reinforced by a cold airmass, and our \nnext cold front is slated to move through the CWA this Saturday \nand Sunday. Our active pattern may continue into next week, as \nlong range models attempt to bring another cold front to the \nregion next Tuesday.\n\nHigh temperatures remain in the 60s areawide both Thursday and \nFriday, but may drop to the 50s, and even upper 40s, depending on\nthe speed of the cold front on Saturday. Next week and beyond, the\nlatest NBM and WPC temperature data suggest that we may stay below\naverage in the 40s and 50s during the day. Meanwhile, for our\nmorning lows, we are forecast to return back to freezing \ntemperatures Sunday morning. The mornings next week may stay in \nthe 30s range, but it will be difficult to remain sub-freezing due\nto our potential quality moisture surge. That being said, our \nnext shot for precipitation is expected next Monday and Tuesday. \nCurrently, the track of our next upper level low favors the \nPanhandles for precipitation, but more model consistency will need\nto be realized before PoPs can reasonably increase for that time \nframe.\n\nRangel",
